course: dictionary
lesson: 15260
title: Shrunken and shrink
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Shrunken» is an adjective, a describing word. It tells you that something has become smaller than it was before. Its root word is «shrink», which means to get smaller.
explain: «The shrunken apple sat wrinkled in the bowl, dry and tiny after a month on the shelf.» The apple used to be round and juicy. Now it is smaller and dry, so shrunken fits. Check by swapping in smaller: the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Shrunken can also describe numbers or amounts. «The shrunken budget meant the school had less money to spend.» Nothing here is wrinkled or dry. The clue is less money, so shrunken means made smaller in amount.
fact: «Shrunken» means having become smaller than before.
fact: Shrunken often looks wrinkled, dried or weak.
fact: «Shrink» is the root word, with the forms shrink, shrank, shrunk and shrunken.
fact: «Shrunken» can also mean reduced, as in a shrunken amount of money or food.
fact: A jumper washed too hot can come out shrunken, so it no longer fits.
exercise: «The old man was shrunken and frail, and he walked slowly with a stick.» What does shrunken mean here? | Loud and cheerful | *Smaller and weaker than before | Taller and stronger than before | Painted bright red
exercise: «After a hot wash, Sam's jumper was shrunken, so it no longer fitted him.» Find and copy the word that tells you the jumper became smaller. | *shrunken | fitted | washed | jumper
exercise: «The spell left the prince shrunken to the size of a mouse.» What happened to the prince? | *He became much smaller | He turned into gold | He became very loud | He grew a foot taller
test: «The shrunken raisins were dry and wrinkled.» What does shrunken mean? | *Made smaller and dried up | Cold from the fridge | Very large and shiny | Sweet and sticky
test: «The shrunken profits meant the shop had less money this year.» What does shrunken mean in this sentence? | Painted in bright colours | Hidden away under the counter | *Made smaller, reduced | Larger and richer
exam: Ruby says shrunken means very big. Is she right? | Yes, it means very big | *No, it means smaller than before | Yes, it means bright and shiny | No, it means very happy
